# MONITORING-LOG-COVERAGE-ANCHOR-INJECTION-1 — log coverage authority

Extends [113-monitoring-logs-watch-truthfulness](113-monitoring-logs-watch-truthfulness.mdc),
[114-monitoring-log-source-authority](114-monitoring-log-source-authority.mdc) and
[115-monitoring-log-timestamp-authority](115-monitoring-log-timestamp-authority.mdc).

Rule 114 settled **which files** the monitor may read. Rule 115 settled **what an
event's timestamp means** once a line has been read. This rule settles the question
those two leave open: **how the monitor knows it read enough.**

## The defect this rule exists to prevent

A source larger than the scan budget is read tail-only, so its prefix is skipped.
Coverage used to be granted when the oldest event timestamp *inside the scanned tail*
already sat before the lookback cutoff — reasoning that the skipped prefix must be
older still. That silently assumed a strictly chronological file, and it made log
**content** the authority over bytes the monitor never opened.

Reproduced end to end: a real in-window `pilot.ERROR` in the unread prefix, one line
beginning `[2019-01-01 00:00:00]` in the tail, and the section reported **OK** with the
reason *"No fresh error events within lookback window."* Two files identical but for
that single line gave WATCH and OK respectively.

## R1 — Coverage is a fact about the read, never about its contents

A log source counts as covered only when the read began at byte 0. No parsed event, no
timestamp, no line count and no message may contribute to that decision.

**An event cannot testify about bytes nobody read.**

## R2 — Event time and scan coverage are separate authorities

`oldest_scanned_event_at` answers *"how far back did the scan happen to see?"*. That is
an observation about lines that were read. It must never be used to answer *"was the
unscanned region safe?"*. Conflating the two is the defect, not an implementation
detail of it.

## R3 — The coverage decision must be structurally unable to see content

`MonitoringLogScanCoverage` takes byte offsets and nothing else. Do not add a
`CarbonInterface`, a parsed event, or a line to its constructor or to `isComplete()` in
order to "improve" coverage. Behaviour can be re-broken by a later edit; a type that
cannot accept a timestamp cannot be re-broken by accident, and a test pins that.

## R4 — A partially read required source is never OK

If any required source was read from a non-zero offset and the skipped bytes could hold
an event inside the active observation window, the logs section must not report OK.
Withdraw the coverage claim and say which bytes were skipped.

Do not substitute a monotonic-ordering argument for a read. Laravel logs are written by
several processes (PHP-FPM workers and the queue worker), may be appended with explicit
past timestamps, and may be concatenated by restore or rotation. Strict chronology is
not an invariant the monitor may rely on.

## R5 — Caps are safety limits, and reaching one costs coverage

The scan budget is a bound on work, not a licence to assume the remainder is clean.
When the cap prevents the monitor from proving full coverage, the claim is withdrawn
and the section fails closed. Raising the budget is the operator's lever
(`foundation_monitoring.log_scan.max_source_bytes`); it is clamped so that buying
coverage can never become an unbounded read.

## R6 — Multi-source coverage is conjunctive

For a `stack`, or for a `daily` channel whose window spans several day-files, coverage
is complete only when **every** required source produced a valid coverage result. One
source never certifies another: an ancient line in today's file says nothing about
yesterday's unread bytes, and a complete read of one stack member says nothing about an
unsupported, missing or unreadable sibling.

## R7 — Valid but unexpected content must not manufacture a claim

Log content is not trusted input. Laravel builds its `LineFormatter` with
`allowInlineLineBreaks: true`, so a newline inside a **message or a context array
value** becomes a real physical line — and any line starting `[Y-m-d H:i:s]` is read as
an event header. Forged headers are therefore reachable wherever unbounded text (an
exception message, a driver error carrying SQL and bindings) reaches a log call.

This rule does not forbid that; it makes it harmless to coverage. A monitor must stay
correct in the presence of syntactically valid content it did not expect, whatever the
content's origin — no public unauthenticated attacker is required for this to matter.

## R8 — Fixes address the invariant, not the sample payload

Ignoring `INFO`, trusting only `ERROR`, or filtering a known string would leave content
in control of the decision and merely raise the price of the same exploit. Fix what is
allowed to *decide*, never which payload happens to be in the reproducer.

## R9 — Both directions are pinned

Fail-closed alone is not success. A monitor that can never go green is its own defect
(rule 113 R3). Every change here keeps a proven-recoverable path: a fully read source
with no fresh errors reports OK, one fresh error reports WATCH, and 150 fresh errors
still escalate.
